`sum` is not defined in Oro — a builtin takes scalars and a collection method takes a collection: use `xs.sum()`
`sorted` is not defined in Oro — a builtin takes scalars and a collection method takes a collection: use `xs.sort_by(x => x)`, or `xs.sort_by(f, reverse=true)` with a key
`any` is not defined in Oro — a builtin takes scalars and a collection method takes a collection: use `xs.any(p)`, or `xs.any(x => x)` for truthiness
`all` is not defined in Oro — a builtin takes scalars and a collection method takes a collection: use `xs.all(p)`, or `xs.all(x => x)` for truthiness
`enumerate` is not in Oro — every `for` yields (index, value), so write `for i, x in xs` (and `for _, x in xs` when the index is unused)
`zip` is not defined in Oro — a builtin takes scalars and a collection method takes a collection: use `a.zip(b)`, which takes any number of further sequences
---
1 3 0.5 b
min() takes two or more values in Oro — a builtin takes scalars and a collection method takes a collection, so the whole-sequence form is `xs.min()`. `min(a, b)` is unchanged.
max() takes two or more values in Oro — a builtin takes scalars and a collection method takes a collection, so the whole-sequence form is `xs.max()`. `max(a, b)` is unchanged.
